<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Voivodeship of Transylvania</h1><p class="st-meta">1176 CE – 1570 CE (approx.) · capital Gyulafehérvár</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Eastern province of medieval Hungary ruled by royal voivodes, home to Hungarian nobles, Saxons and Székelys, which became an autonomous principality in 1570.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After Stephen I annexed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/realm-of-the-gyulas">Realm of the Gyulas</a> in 1003, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-transylvania">Transylvania</a> was organised in royal counties. From 1176, when Leustach is named voivode, a royal official with that Slavic title governed the province for the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The voivode headed the counties of the nobility, while German settlers (<a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saxons">Saxons</a>) had their own rights under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diploma-andreanum">Andreanum</a> of 1224 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szekelys">Székelys</a> formed a separate military community. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> formed much of the peasantry. Voivodes such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-kan">Ladislaus Kán</a> around 1300 acted almost as independent rulers. After a peasant revolt in 1437, nobles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> and Székelys formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-three-nations">Union of Three Nations</a>, which excluded Romanians. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a> served as voivode from 1441.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Mohács</a> in 1526, voivode <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-zapolya">John Zápolya</a> became king of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-sigismund-zapolya">John Sigismund</a> renounced the royal title by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-speyer">Speyer</a> in 1570, and the land became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-transylvania">Principality of Transylvania</a> under Ottoman overlordship.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/voivodeship-of-transylvania.jpg" alt="Voivodeship of Transylvania" width="320" height="208"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coa_Hungary_Country_History_med_(1915).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1176 CE – 1570 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gyulafehérvár</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ladislaus Kán, John Hunyadi, John Zápolya</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity; Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hungarian; German; Romanian; Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Transylvania (medieval), Transylvanian Voivodeship</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1373&amp;focus=voivodeship-of-transylvania" aria-label="Voivodeship of Transylvania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5291 562 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Voivodeship of Transylvania lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5446 783h0M5446 783h0M5445 769h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5611 818h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5611 818h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Romania. <a href="/atlas?year=1373&amp;focus=voivodeship-of-transylvania">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
